          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I had flu two weeks ago and I am still exhausted. Is prolonged tiredness afterwards a concern, and what does guidance describe about antivirals and vaccination?",
          "answer": "The tiredness you're experiencing after flu for two weeks is categorized as a routine concern. This means there isn't an immediate emergency; however, if your symptoms worsen, or if you develop new, unexplained symptoms, it's important to seek medical advice.\n\nProlonged fatigue after influenza is common and can last for two weeks or more. This fatigue often continues even after other symptoms resolve, and it usually does not indicate a new underlying condition unless additional concerning symptoms arise.\n\nRegarding antiviral medications, they are most effective when initiated within 48 hours of symptom onset. Treatment may reduce the duration of illness slightly, particularly in otherwise healthy individuals. However, guidelines suggest that for those who are hospitalized or at high risk of complications, antiviral treatment is strongly recommended within that time frame. For lower-risk patients, the recommendations can vary; some guidelines consider it reasonable, while others note the benefits are modest and do not support routine use.\n\nAnnual vaccination against influenza is advised for everyone six months and older to help prevent flu and its complications. The vaccine is reformulated each year based on circulating virus strains, and receiving the vaccine later in the season can still be beneficial while the virus is present. For personalized guidance, it's recommended to consult your healthcare provider.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
